WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.212 --> 00:00:06.920
MALE SPEAKER: Hello,
everyone and welcome

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:08.640
to this afternoon's webinar.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.830
My name's Greg Wilson.

00:00:09.830 --> 00:00:12.710
I work for RStudio on
the education team.

00:00:12.710 --> 00:00:15.080
And we've put this one
together to help everybody

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:17.900
who was finding themselves
moving their teaching online

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.267
at short notice.

00:00:19.267 --> 00:00:21.350
I'd like to thank Robert
and Curtis for setting up

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:23.240
all of the mechanics for this.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:27.080
And there is a question
tab in GoToWebinar,

00:00:27.080 --> 00:00:29.630
please throw questions
into it at any time

00:00:29.630 --> 00:00:31.670
during the presentation
we'll field some of them

00:00:31.670 --> 00:00:36.320
during the call and we'll
gather up the rest at the end.

00:00:36.320 --> 00:00:37.040
All right.

00:00:37.040 --> 00:00:40.510
Let's get rolling because it's
a big world and a short day.

00:00:40.510 --> 00:00:45.050
So you all had
plans, you all had

00:00:45.050 --> 00:00:46.982
some idea of how you
were going to teach

00:00:46.982 --> 00:00:49.190
a course this semester, how
you were going to deliver

00:00:49.190 --> 00:00:52.040
that training workshop,
and now all of a sudden,

00:00:52.040 --> 00:00:55.220
you find yourself in an
off road vehicle at night.

00:00:55.220 --> 00:00:57.500
We've all of us been
thrown off our plans

00:00:57.500 --> 00:00:59.090
in the last couple of weeks.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.160
And quite reasonably,
a lot of us

00:01:01.160 --> 00:01:05.160
were afraid that things are
going to crash out that.

00:01:05.160 --> 00:01:06.880
We're here to help.

00:01:06.880 --> 00:01:09.760
What we're going to do
in the next 45 minutes

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:12.130
is give you a few
tips that we hope

00:01:12.130 --> 00:01:18.380
will help you move your teaching
online without crashing.

00:01:18.380 --> 00:01:21.140
It's not the best you
could possibly do,

00:01:21.140 --> 00:01:23.900
it's what you can do in a
hurry with the tools you've got

00:01:23.900 --> 00:01:25.940
and the time you have.

00:01:25.940 --> 00:01:28.010
Because like your
learners, you're

00:01:28.010 --> 00:01:30.110
probably now wrestling
with a whole bunch

00:01:30.110 --> 00:01:33.260
of issues around looking
after kids or elderly parents

00:01:33.260 --> 00:01:35.960
travel plans being
canceled, everybody

00:01:35.960 --> 00:01:39.710
and their cousin being on
bandwidth at the same time.

00:01:39.710 --> 00:01:42.590
We're hoping that these tips
will help you get through that.

00:01:42.590 --> 00:01:44.840
Now this is the
persona I had in mind

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:47.840
when I developed this deck.

00:01:47.840 --> 00:01:51.560
Emily's pre-tenure your faculty
also teaches in industry.

00:01:51.560 --> 00:01:54.420
She's comfortable
using modern tools.

00:01:54.420 --> 00:01:56.780
She FaceTimes with her
partner all the time.

00:01:56.780 --> 00:01:59.570
She struggles a bit with her
school's learning management

00:01:59.570 --> 00:02:03.083
system, but that's blackboards
fault, that's not hers.

00:02:03.083 --> 00:02:05.000
And now she's got to
move a class of 80 people

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:07.640
online in a week so that
they can finish semester

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:08.990
more or less on time.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:10.699
And she's also got a
couple of workshops

00:02:10.699 --> 00:02:12.620
that she signed contracts for.

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.140
She really doesn't want to
have to cancel out of those.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:17.480
And above all of
this, she is afraid

00:02:17.480 --> 00:02:20.180
that whatever happens in
the next few weeks is just

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.490
going to suck a lot of
time away from research,

00:02:22.490 --> 00:02:25.760
and she's a year away
from applying for tenure.

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:28.760
And I put this concept map
together in order to figure out

00:02:28.760 --> 00:02:30.530
what I was going to cover.

00:02:30.530 --> 00:02:32.390
I wound up covering
more than this,

00:02:32.390 --> 00:02:35.250
so I'm going to skip and go to
the first and most important

00:02:35.250 --> 00:02:35.750
rule.

00:02:35.750 --> 00:02:37.880
And if you take one thing
away from today's talk,

00:02:37.880 --> 00:02:41.900
I want it to be this, don't
change what you don't have to.

00:02:41.900 --> 00:02:44.150
There's an old saying, if
you're going on a long walk,

00:02:44.150 --> 00:02:45.710
you wear old shoes.

00:02:45.710 --> 00:02:48.230
You don't wear new shoes if
you've got a long way to go.

00:02:48.230 --> 00:02:51.230
Please don't try to
change 19 different things

00:02:51.230 --> 00:02:54.170
about your class because
you've heard that the right way

00:02:54.170 --> 00:02:57.620
to teach online is
to use x or do y.

00:02:57.620 --> 00:03:02.480
Right now, running late, running
without extra resources, what

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:05.510
we want to look for is the
smallest possible changes

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:06.570
we can make.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.840
And that's what I'm
going to be focusing on.

00:03:09.840 --> 00:03:11.340
The second rule,
and this one comes

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.770
from Elizabeth Wickes at
the University of Illinois,

00:03:13.770 --> 00:03:16.080
please don't make assumptions.

00:03:16.080 --> 00:03:19.530
Please ask your learners, your
students, your colleagues.

00:03:19.530 --> 00:03:23.220
They aren't going to have the
same bandwidth that you have.

00:03:23.220 --> 00:03:25.490
Their network might
not be as reliable.

00:03:25.490 --> 00:03:28.530
Their computer at home might
be a six-year-old hand-me-down

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.900
because they're no
longer at the university.

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:34.260
In a corporate environment, it
might be the very first time

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:36.570
they've had to use
stuff over a VPN

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:39.420
because they're used to
going into the office.

00:03:39.420 --> 00:03:41.940
And they may not have
the attention span

00:03:41.940 --> 00:03:45.720
they used to because of all
of these pandemic-induced

00:03:45.720 --> 00:03:47.070
distractions.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:51.060
So rule number two, go and
ask and nobody will ever

00:03:51.060 --> 00:03:54.690
be offended if you say, what
kind of device are you on,

00:03:54.690 --> 00:03:58.900
how much time do you
realistically have.

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:01.720
The next recommendation
I've got is

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:06.310
please don't try to be or to
beat the kind of recorded video

00:04:06.310 --> 00:04:11.800
you see in polished produce
courses on sites like EdX.

00:04:11.800 --> 00:04:17.040
It takes a lot of time to
edit up even a simple video,

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:19.200
and it takes a lot of time
to learn the tools you

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:21.269
use for editing those videos.

00:04:21.269 --> 00:04:24.360
You probably don't have
that time right now.

00:04:24.360 --> 00:04:27.480
I'm not saying that recorded
video doesn't have its place,

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:29.040
we're going to
record this webinar

00:04:29.040 --> 00:04:31.530
and make it available to people.

00:04:31.530 --> 00:04:34.460
But right now, I think
in the short run,

00:04:34.460 --> 00:04:38.840
your best bet is to try to use
things interactively and worry

00:04:38.840 --> 00:04:44.790
about recording and editing
and publishing video later.

00:04:44.790 --> 00:04:46.620
If you're going to
ignore my advice

00:04:46.620 --> 00:04:48.840
and go ahead and start
doing it, there's

00:04:48.840 --> 00:04:52.830
a really good paper from 2009
by Chen &amp;amp; Rabb called A Pattern

00:04:52.830 --> 00:04:55.170
Language for Screencasting.

00:04:55.170 --> 00:04:59.100
They take a lot of, apparently,
straightforward ideas

00:04:59.100 --> 00:05:03.180
and organize them
in a sensible way.

00:05:03.180 --> 00:05:05.820
Their concept map is
up here on the screen.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:07.920
There's just a whole
bunch of good tips

00:05:07.920 --> 00:05:11.070
here about what makes for
a good screencast, what

00:05:11.070 --> 00:05:14.820
makes for a good online video,
and how the parts fit together

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:18.690
so you know what not to do
or what not to venture into.

00:05:18.690 --> 00:05:20.640
All of these slides,
by the way are going

00:05:20.640 --> 00:05:22.870
to be available after the talk.

00:05:22.870 --> 00:05:24.990
So if you haven't
copy down that URL,

00:05:24.990 --> 00:05:29.140
don't worry you'll be
able to find it later.

00:05:29.140 --> 00:05:34.200
Next thing I'm going to say is
the mistakes are the pedagogy.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.110
Depending on whose
numbers you trust,

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.930
a professional programmer
spends 20% to 60%

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:41.850
of their time debugging.

00:05:41.850 --> 00:05:45.780
A novice spends 80% to 95%
of their time debugging

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:48.010
because they're a novice.

00:05:48.010 --> 00:05:51.920
The same is going to
be true in most areas.

00:05:51.920 --> 00:05:55.000
So please don't worry
about making mistakes

00:05:55.000 --> 00:05:56.620
in front of your
learners when you're

00:05:56.620 --> 00:06:00.280
teaching online for the first
or the second or the third time.

00:06:00.280 --> 00:06:02.020
Because every time
you make a mistake

00:06:02.020 --> 00:06:05.140
and then say, oh, this is wrong.

00:06:05.140 --> 00:06:08.410
Here's how I can tell, here's
how I figured out how to fix it

00:06:08.410 --> 00:06:10.480
and here's how I can
tell it's been fixed.

00:06:10.480 --> 00:06:13.580
They will have learned
something valuable.

00:06:13.580 --> 00:06:16.040
And this comes back
to the larger point

00:06:16.040 --> 00:06:18.800
that when you're teaching online
interactively, when you're

00:06:18.800 --> 00:06:22.670
doing what I'm doing right
now, you learn the song

00:06:22.670 --> 00:06:25.940
but then you improvise over it.

00:06:25.940 --> 00:06:28.610
I've got a slide deck,
it has speaker's notes,

00:06:28.610 --> 00:06:30.545
I'm really glad that
I prepped all of that

00:06:30.545 --> 00:06:32.420
because it got me to
think through the things

00:06:32.420 --> 00:06:34.010
I'm going to say.

00:06:34.010 --> 00:06:36.860
But now what I'm actually
saying on any given day

00:06:36.860 --> 00:06:39.110
is going to be
partly improvised.

00:06:39.110 --> 00:06:40.610
It's going to depend
on the feedback

00:06:40.610 --> 00:06:42.120
that I'm getting
from my audience.

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:44.720
It's going to depend on thoughts
that I have now that I'm

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:47.588
presenting, and that's OK.

00:06:47.588 --> 00:06:49.880
The better you know your
material, the more comfortable

00:06:49.880 --> 00:06:51.740
you will be improvising.

00:06:51.740 --> 00:06:54.538
But don't think that you have
to stick to exactly what you

00:06:54.538 --> 00:06:55.580
planned the night before.

00:06:58.770 --> 00:07:03.110
And the corollary to this
is it's really, really hard

00:07:03.110 --> 00:07:06.870
to teach to a roomful
of empty chairs.

00:07:06.870 --> 00:07:11.340
All of us, all the time,
are picking up social cues.

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:14.310
We're constantly peripherally
aware of whether somebody

00:07:14.310 --> 00:07:17.010
is nodding, or whether
they're checking their phone,

00:07:17.010 --> 00:07:19.620
did they just yawn or are
they thinking because they're

00:07:19.620 --> 00:07:21.240
about to ask a question.

00:07:21.240 --> 00:07:25.620
You might not notice that
you're noticing but you notice.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:28.590
And as soon as you start
teaching online, a lot of that

00:07:28.590 --> 00:07:30.570
is taken away.

00:07:30.570 --> 00:07:33.650
If you take all
of it away, you're

00:07:33.650 --> 00:07:37.850
going to find it very,
very difficult to teach.

00:07:37.850 --> 00:07:41.750
What I suggest you do
is get your students

00:07:41.750 --> 00:07:46.340
into some sort of
webinar like this,

00:07:46.340 --> 00:07:49.280
but then have a few of
them turn on their video

00:07:49.280 --> 00:07:52.100
so that you can
see a few people.

00:07:52.100 --> 00:07:55.190
If, for example, you're using
Zoom, which is my preferred

00:07:55.190 --> 00:07:59.710
video conferencing tool, pick
a half a dozen names at random

00:07:59.710 --> 00:08:03.280
and ask them to turn on video,
and then focus your attention

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:05.410
on those six.

00:08:05.410 --> 00:08:09.160
Ask them your questions, pay
attention to their faces,

00:08:09.160 --> 00:08:12.580
just as you would in a real
class, and in your next class

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:15.020
pick a different half dozen.

00:08:15.020 --> 00:08:18.140
This teaching practice
predates video.

00:08:18.140 --> 00:08:19.090
It was quite common.

00:08:19.090 --> 00:08:20.780
It still is in some places.

00:08:20.780 --> 00:08:23.750
If you've got a large
class to pick a few people

00:08:23.750 --> 00:08:27.200
bring them down front and
focus your teaching on them

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.020
for this hour, but then focus
it on a different half dozen

00:08:30.020 --> 00:08:32.299
for the next hour and so on.

00:08:32.299 --> 00:08:34.010
They get individual
attention, you

00:08:34.010 --> 00:08:37.490
get the kind of responsiveness
you need to make improvisation

00:08:37.490 --> 00:08:40.210
and teaching lively.

00:08:40.210 --> 00:08:42.130
And everybody else
now has permission

00:08:42.130 --> 00:08:45.730
to turn off the video and
reduce the load on the system.

00:08:45.730 --> 00:08:47.770
If you're going
to do this, please

00:08:47.770 --> 00:08:50.620
give people a way to opt out.

00:08:50.620 --> 00:08:52.510
For a whole bunch of
reasons, a lot of people

00:08:52.510 --> 00:08:56.990
are uncomfortable
being on video online.

00:08:56.990 --> 00:08:59.530
If you've ever had to deal
with harassment or a stalker,

00:08:59.530 --> 00:09:01.780
you don't want images of
yourself appearing that might

00:09:01.780 --> 00:09:03.190
go out of your control.

00:09:03.190 --> 00:09:05.743
People have many other issues.

00:09:05.743 --> 00:09:07.660
If they're working at
home for the first time,

00:09:07.660 --> 00:09:09.790
for example, maybe
they don't want

00:09:09.790 --> 00:09:12.190
others to be able to see
the mess in the background,

00:09:12.190 --> 00:09:14.830
the kids playing, and so on.

00:09:14.830 --> 00:09:19.370
So invite people, don't mandate
it, but try to get, as I said,

00:09:19.370 --> 00:09:20.980
for five or six
people that you're

00:09:20.980 --> 00:09:23.080
directing your teaching to.

00:09:23.080 --> 00:09:25.030
That will keep you
engaged and you'll

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:28.630
do a better job with teaching.

00:09:28.630 --> 00:09:32.390
The next one was recommended
by one of my colleagues.

00:09:32.390 --> 00:09:35.080
And I'm embarrassed that I
didn't think of it because it

00:09:35.080 --> 00:09:37.870
reveals my expert blindspot.

00:09:37.870 --> 00:09:40.210
The first time you
move your class online,

00:09:40.210 --> 00:09:43.990
give them a tour of the
tool that you're using.

00:09:43.990 --> 00:09:48.010
They probably haven't seen
Zoom or whatever you've got.

00:09:48.010 --> 00:09:51.700
They might not know where
to click the button to mute.

00:09:51.700 --> 00:09:54.310
Seems obvious once
you know it's anything

00:09:54.310 --> 00:09:57.440
but if you've just
been thrown into it.

00:09:57.440 --> 00:10:00.190
So taking 10 minutes at the
start of your first online

00:10:00.190 --> 00:10:02.890
lecture to show
people how you're

00:10:02.890 --> 00:10:05.080
expecting them to use the tool.

00:10:05.080 --> 00:10:09.970
Or taking an hour beforehand
to take a few screenshots

00:10:09.970 --> 00:10:14.650
and then share those with the
class can save a lot of grief.

00:10:14.650 --> 00:10:17.080
If you're going to
do this, make sure

00:10:17.080 --> 00:10:20.860
that you're logged in as a
normal user, not as the teacher

00:10:20.860 --> 00:10:25.190
or the conference organizer
when you're giving the tool,

00:10:25.190 --> 00:10:28.660
because if you're logged
in as a super user

00:10:28.660 --> 00:10:32.065
you will see a different set
of tools that they don't have

00:10:32.065 --> 00:10:33.190
and that will confuse them.

00:10:36.130 --> 00:10:40.390
The next one up is
to direct traffic.

00:10:40.390 --> 00:10:42.700
You don't have to go
full North Korean, that's

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:44.260
where this pictures from.

00:10:44.260 --> 00:10:46.570
But again, for a
lot of people, this

00:10:46.570 --> 00:10:49.030
is their first time
being in some sort

00:10:49.030 --> 00:10:51.760
of structured formal
setting online.

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:55.060
So always give them verbal cues.

00:10:55.060 --> 00:10:58.060
Ask them to put their
questions in the chat.

00:10:58.060 --> 00:11:02.290
Ask them to add their answers
to a homework question

00:11:02.290 --> 00:11:04.160
to the shared Google Doc.

00:11:04.160 --> 00:11:08.170
Call on them by name when
you want them to speak.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:11.230
Or ask them to type
something like slash hand

00:11:11.230 --> 00:11:16.090
or raise hand in the chat
when they want to speak.

00:11:16.090 --> 00:11:19.300
Make sure that they're all
getting direction about what

00:11:19.300 --> 00:11:20.500
they're supposed to do next.

00:11:23.190 --> 00:11:26.660
After a while, you
can dial that down.

00:11:26.660 --> 00:11:29.730
But I have found in my own
teaching that I don't--

00:11:29.730 --> 00:11:32.970
I constantly do it
because I've constantly

00:11:32.970 --> 00:11:35.460
got people who are new
or who are coming in

00:11:35.460 --> 00:11:38.670
from somebody else's class
where they might have evolved

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:41.130
a different set of conventions.

00:11:41.130 --> 00:11:44.820
Whatever it is, you, as the
tour guide, telling people now

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:47.500
we're moving on to the
Renaissance art section,

00:11:47.500 --> 00:11:49.470
everybody step this way.

00:11:49.470 --> 00:11:52.750
Again, it takes away a lot of
the uncertainty and right now,

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.750
people have got enough
uncertainty to deal with.

00:11:58.290 --> 00:12:03.210
One other point on this,
mute early and mute often.

00:12:03.210 --> 00:12:06.600
By default now when I'm
in Zoom, after somebody

00:12:06.600 --> 00:12:10.260
finishes speaking, I don't
wait for them to mute.

00:12:10.260 --> 00:12:13.290
I don't even mute
them, I instinctively

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:15.720
now hit the mute all button.

00:12:15.720 --> 00:12:18.120
I put everybody back
in a muted state

00:12:18.120 --> 00:12:21.670
even if they don't need to
be because that way I never

00:12:21.670 --> 00:12:23.930
forget to.

00:12:23.930 --> 00:12:27.920
You can do a very
good class audio-only

00:12:27.920 --> 00:12:29.570
or audio with slides.

00:12:29.570 --> 00:12:33.430
Video is helpful, but
it's not essential.

00:12:33.430 --> 00:12:35.120
And I can prove
this by asking, what

00:12:35.120 --> 00:12:38.330
would happen if we left you with
video of me talking right now

00:12:38.330 --> 00:12:41.150
but turned off the sound?

00:12:41.150 --> 00:12:43.520
You wouldn't learn anything.

00:12:43.520 --> 00:12:47.162
You've got to make sure
that everybody can be heard.

00:12:47.162 --> 00:12:48.620
You've got to make
sure that you're

00:12:48.620 --> 00:12:52.280
not drowned out by feedback,
by background noise,

00:12:52.280 --> 00:12:55.730
or crucially, by somebody
who constantly wants

00:12:55.730 --> 00:12:57.420
to be the next one
to ask the question,

00:12:57.420 --> 00:12:59.300
and constantly wants
to jump in first.

00:13:02.010 --> 00:13:05.820
One thing that I do
to ensure that is I

00:13:05.820 --> 00:13:09.565
actually keep a written list of
names beside me of the people

00:13:09.565 --> 00:13:10.940
that I'm focusing
my teaching on,

00:13:10.940 --> 00:13:13.860
that half dozen I
referred to earlier.

00:13:13.860 --> 00:13:15.750
Every time I interact
with them, I just

00:13:15.750 --> 00:13:18.810
put a tick beside their name
so that I can very quickly

00:13:18.810 --> 00:13:21.540
see who I have been
talking to and who

00:13:21.540 --> 00:13:24.050
I haven't been talking to.

00:13:24.050 --> 00:13:27.170
That way, I can distribute my
attention more or less fairly

00:13:27.170 --> 00:13:29.270
among those half dozen.

00:13:29.270 --> 00:13:32.630
And if somebody is
constantly trying to jump in,

00:13:32.630 --> 00:13:35.390
I can say no, it's
not your turn.

00:13:35.390 --> 00:13:37.760
We're trying to take turns here.

00:13:37.760 --> 00:13:41.480
If you are relying on people to
unmute themselves and just jump

00:13:41.480 --> 00:13:43.740
in with their
questions, your class

00:13:43.740 --> 00:13:46.790
is going to be dominated
by a couple of extroverts.

00:13:46.790 --> 00:13:48.440
And everybody else
will very quickly

00:13:48.440 --> 00:13:51.440
learn to not even bother
to try to ask questions

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:53.750
or to contribute ideas
because they can never

00:13:53.750 --> 00:13:55.730
get in fast enough.

00:13:55.730 --> 00:13:57.510
Mute early, mute often.

00:13:57.510 --> 00:14:00.300
And then have people request
the microphone in order

00:14:00.300 --> 00:14:04.080
to speak so that
you do the unmuting.

00:14:04.080 --> 00:14:06.760
And as I said call
on people by name.

00:14:06.760 --> 00:14:11.520
If I ask the room, are
there any questions?

00:14:11.520 --> 00:14:14.190
It will be followed
with silence.

00:14:14.190 --> 00:14:17.760
Sometimes that silence means
nobody has any questions.

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:20.460
Sometimes that silence
means people have questions,

00:14:20.460 --> 00:14:22.320
but they don't want
to look foolish.

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:26.380
And sometimes that silence
means they're very busy typing,

00:14:26.380 --> 00:14:29.200
and the question is going
to pop up in 15 seconds

00:14:29.200 --> 00:14:33.350
by which point, I will
probably have moved on.

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:37.100
Instead when I want
somebody to contribute,

00:14:37.100 --> 00:14:39.685
I call on them by name.

00:14:39.685 --> 00:14:41.060
And if they want
to pass, if they

00:14:41.060 --> 00:14:42.518
say I don't have
an answer, I don't

00:14:42.518 --> 00:14:44.960
feel like this is the
right time, that's fine,

00:14:44.960 --> 00:14:47.530
I'll move on to the next person.

00:14:47.530 --> 00:14:50.530
Calling on people
by name also helps

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:53.710
you level out your classroom.

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:57.430
You are about to discover
that some of your learners

00:14:57.430 --> 00:15:00.080
think much more
quickly than others.

00:15:00.080 --> 00:15:02.530
That doesn't mean
they're smarter.

00:15:02.530 --> 00:15:05.060
It doesn't mean they're
producing better work,

00:15:05.060 --> 00:15:10.110
it just means that their
first response comes faster.

00:15:10.110 --> 00:15:12.640
If you are calling
on people by name,

00:15:12.640 --> 00:15:14.900
you can even that out
by throwing softballs

00:15:14.900 --> 00:15:16.650
to people who might
need a little bit more

00:15:16.650 --> 00:15:18.960
time, throwing harder
questions at people

00:15:18.960 --> 00:15:20.850
who are a little quicker.

00:15:20.850 --> 00:15:22.830
And that way the whole
class is progressing

00:15:22.830 --> 00:15:23.820
at about the same rate.

00:15:27.250 --> 00:15:29.690
This is all a lot to manage.

00:15:29.690 --> 00:15:33.450
So this brings us to
the other big takeaway.

00:15:33.450 --> 00:15:34.680
Don't teach alone.

00:15:34.680 --> 00:15:39.170
If you can possibly avoid
it, don't be the only person

00:15:39.170 --> 00:15:40.700
trying to run things.

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:44.520
Every great surgeon has
a great nurse beside her.

00:15:44.520 --> 00:15:47.110
Every great teacher
has a co-teacher.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:49.740
It could be a fellow
instructor, it

00:15:49.740 --> 00:15:53.340
could be a TA, it could
be one of your learners

00:15:53.340 --> 00:15:55.320
that you've
approached to say, you

00:15:55.320 --> 00:15:57.990
seem to be OK with
this material,

00:15:57.990 --> 00:15:59.460
can I ask you a favor?

00:15:59.460 --> 00:16:02.640
Whoever it is, you
want somebody else

00:16:02.640 --> 00:16:05.340
keeping track of whose
turn it is to speak next.

00:16:05.340 --> 00:16:07.920
Somebody else making
sure that the slides

00:16:07.920 --> 00:16:09.270
are presenting properly.

00:16:09.270 --> 00:16:11.623
Somebody else who
is wrangling that

00:16:11.623 --> 00:16:13.290
shared document that
we're going to talk

00:16:13.290 --> 00:16:15.360
about in a couple of minutes.

00:16:15.360 --> 00:16:19.020
You want a co-pilot who's
worrying about everything that

00:16:19.020 --> 00:16:22.470
isn't the actual delivery
of the lesson in the moment

00:16:22.470 --> 00:16:26.680
to free you up to focus on your
slides and what you're saying.

00:16:26.680 --> 00:16:31.140
I've got that right now, and
it makes a world of difference.

00:16:31.140 --> 00:16:33.720
It doesn't take
a lot of practice

00:16:33.720 --> 00:16:36.290
to learn how to do that.

00:16:36.290 --> 00:16:41.420
And it does also mean
that if next week you're

00:16:41.420 --> 00:16:46.080
unable to make a scheduled
class, for whatever reason,

00:16:46.080 --> 00:16:48.140
there's somebody else
who's at least partly

00:16:48.140 --> 00:16:50.240
familiar with what
you're teaching

00:16:50.240 --> 00:16:51.910
and with how you're
delivering it.

00:16:54.730 --> 00:16:55.710
So do a dry run.

00:16:59.300 --> 00:17:03.050
I have a list some place of
things that have gone wrong

00:17:03.050 --> 00:17:05.260
while I've been teaching.

00:17:05.260 --> 00:17:07.609
I've fallen off stage
twice and concussed myself.

00:17:07.609 --> 00:17:09.700
Once I've had a
fistfight break out,

00:17:09.700 --> 00:17:14.887
I've had a projector catch fire,
I've had a woman go into labor.

00:17:14.887 --> 00:17:17.304
And I once actually fell asleep
in one of my own lectures.

00:17:21.000 --> 00:17:22.380
Those are the easy ones.

00:17:22.380 --> 00:17:25.710
The hard one is nobody told
me there was a firewall.

00:17:25.710 --> 00:17:29.490
So all of a sudden, I can't
get any of the data sets.

00:17:29.490 --> 00:17:32.940
Nobody told me that they've
updated Zoom and some

00:17:32.940 --> 00:17:35.970
of the controls have moved
or behave differently.

00:17:35.970 --> 00:17:40.770
Or I know all of that, but
I've never done it with Robert.

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:42.990
I've never done it with Karina.

00:17:42.990 --> 00:17:47.160
And so we're used to using
the tool in different ways.

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:49.770
Getting 10 or 15
minutes, half an hour

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:52.230
before you're supposed
to start talking

00:17:52.230 --> 00:17:54.690
can make a world of
difference to your delivery

00:17:54.690 --> 00:17:57.422
and it can lower your blood
pressure considerably.

00:18:01.490 --> 00:18:05.360
Another small note, keep
your notes the side you.

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:11.930
I've got a laptop on
my desk, and then I've

00:18:11.930 --> 00:18:13.490
got a large screen
monitor above it.

00:18:13.490 --> 00:18:16.730
I think that's a fairly
common setup these days.

00:18:16.730 --> 00:18:19.600
What it meant was
that going back

00:18:19.600 --> 00:18:22.360
a while whenever I was
teaching I was looking up

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:24.513
and have my head tilted back.

00:18:24.513 --> 00:18:25.930
And my daughter
eventually told me

00:18:25.930 --> 00:18:27.305
that as far as
she was concerned,

00:18:27.305 --> 00:18:29.290
my webcam was in
those cam and that's

00:18:29.290 --> 00:18:32.860
why I don't have a
picture for this slide.

00:18:32.860 --> 00:18:34.360
If you've got your
notes beside you,

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:36.892
it's very natural to turn
your head to the side,

00:18:36.892 --> 00:18:38.350
check what you're
going to say next

00:18:38.350 --> 00:18:40.870
and come back to your lecture.

00:18:40.870 --> 00:18:42.910
If you've got the
slides up here,

00:18:42.910 --> 00:18:45.760
it's not something
you're used to doing

00:18:45.760 --> 00:18:47.530
and the angle can be
rather unflattering.

00:18:47.530 --> 00:18:53.520
I know it's a small thing,
but the small things add up.

00:18:53.520 --> 00:18:59.960
A much larger thing, please add
speakers notes to your slides.

00:18:59.960 --> 00:19:03.260
You are about to discover
that a lot of your learners

00:19:03.260 --> 00:19:05.360
have trouble seeing or hearing.

00:19:05.360 --> 00:19:08.300
They've got good compensating
strategies or accommodation

00:19:08.300 --> 00:19:10.400
strategies in a live
classroom because they've

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:14.180
got a lifetime of
experience doing this.

00:19:14.180 --> 00:19:16.845
They may not have
the same support

00:19:16.845 --> 00:19:18.595
now that you're bailing
everything online.

00:19:21.110 --> 00:19:24.110
If you put speakers
notes in your slides,

00:19:24.110 --> 00:19:28.150
it makes the slides more
findable because search tools

00:19:28.150 --> 00:19:29.680
know how to index them.

00:19:29.680 --> 00:19:32.980
It makes the slides more usable
because people can understand

00:19:32.980 --> 00:19:36.010
after the fact what you
meant by that picture,

00:19:36.010 --> 00:19:39.380
but crucially, it makes
them more accessible.

00:19:39.380 --> 00:19:43.550
And however hard you
think life is suddenly

00:19:43.550 --> 00:19:46.970
being thrown online,
it's a lot harder

00:19:46.970 --> 00:19:50.240
if you're partially
sighted or fully deaf.

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:52.520
Please don't make life
harder for people who already

00:19:52.520 --> 00:19:53.645
have more to struggle with.

00:19:56.800 --> 00:20:00.370
In fact, using accessibility
tools helps everyone.

00:20:00.370 --> 00:20:04.870
I don't know if you can
see my cursor right now,

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:07.510
but I've used the
accessibility tools on the Mac

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:11.200
to make it as large as possible
so that it's easier for people

00:20:11.200 --> 00:20:13.580
to see on video.

00:20:13.580 --> 00:20:16.040
I do that whenever I'm teaching.

00:20:16.040 --> 00:20:18.860
I use an enlarged cursor
because a small cursor

00:20:18.860 --> 00:20:22.610
in a small window is
really, really hard to see.

00:20:22.610 --> 00:20:25.070
I switched to high
contrast mode so

00:20:25.070 --> 00:20:28.550
that people who might be
sizing down the screen,

00:20:28.550 --> 00:20:32.000
or might be on older devices or
might just have crappy monitors

00:20:32.000 --> 00:20:36.730
have a better chance of being
able to see what's going on.

00:20:36.730 --> 00:20:40.230
All of these tools help
in key casting tools

00:20:40.230 --> 00:20:44.550
that will show people on screen
all of the control keys, option

00:20:44.550 --> 00:20:47.560
keys, and other things
that you're typing.

00:20:47.560 --> 00:20:50.140
You should be using
those anyway when

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:54.040
you're teaching so that somebody
doesn't think you're typing 100

00:20:54.040 --> 00:20:55.600
characters a
second, they realize

00:20:55.600 --> 00:20:58.650
that you hit Tab and
got filing completion.

00:20:58.650 --> 00:21:00.430
KeyCastr is one
that you can use.

00:21:00.430 --> 00:21:01.840
[INAUDIBLE] another.

00:21:01.840 --> 00:21:04.790
There's a lot of
accessibility tools out there.

00:21:04.790 --> 00:21:08.090
Most of them are either
free or very cheap.

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:10.510
Most of them are
fairly easy to set up,

00:21:10.510 --> 00:21:12.700
and they will all help
with your online teaching.

00:21:12.700 --> 00:21:14.590
And when you're
done, keep using them

00:21:14.590 --> 00:21:16.240
when you go back
in the classroom

00:21:16.240 --> 00:21:20.280
because they'll make
life better for everyone.

00:21:20.280 --> 00:21:23.580
This recommendation comes,
again, from Elizabeth Wickes.

00:21:23.580 --> 00:21:27.420
Teach from your smallest screen.

00:21:27.420 --> 00:21:30.840
If I try to take my
half meter wide monitor

00:21:30.840 --> 00:21:34.990
and squeeze it down into
a little video window,

00:21:34.990 --> 00:21:37.520
the video quality is
going to be pretty grainy

00:21:37.520 --> 00:21:40.900
and the bandwidth demands
are going to be pretty heavy.

00:21:40.900 --> 00:21:42.280
I'm not suggesting
that you teach

00:21:42.280 --> 00:21:45.720
from a tiny handheld
device like this,

00:21:45.720 --> 00:21:47.790
but please remember that
most of your learners

00:21:47.790 --> 00:21:52.540
will be using smaller and
older gear than you've got.

00:21:52.540 --> 00:21:55.000
You want to be at the
bottom of the pack

00:21:55.000 --> 00:21:57.520
when you're the ones sending
out the video because that

00:21:57.520 --> 00:22:00.550
has the greatest chance
of working everywhere.

00:22:00.550 --> 00:22:03.430
And a lot of personal
footnote to this,

00:22:03.430 --> 00:22:09.650
it's difficult to teach with an
IDE, when your screen sharing

00:22:09.650 --> 00:22:12.260
because once you've
sized things down,

00:22:12.260 --> 00:22:15.560
all of the controls in the
IDE for different tabs,

00:22:15.560 --> 00:22:17.100
different menus, and so forth.

00:22:17.100 --> 00:22:19.940
All of those things
that are really helpful,

00:22:19.940 --> 00:22:22.040
they're taking up
a lot of space.

00:22:22.040 --> 00:22:25.320
And you don't have a lot
of leftover real estate

00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:27.620
for your own typing.

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:31.100
I have seen people put
RStudio IDE into a mode

00:22:31.100 --> 00:22:35.220
where only one pane is
being shown at a time.

00:22:35.220 --> 00:22:37.830
That's great from a real
estate point of view,

00:22:37.830 --> 00:22:40.930
but now it looks very different
from what people are used to.

00:22:40.930 --> 00:22:42.960
I don't have a
solution for this one,

00:22:42.960 --> 00:22:44.460
ask your learners
what they prefer.

00:22:47.240 --> 00:22:50.150
Circling back to a topic
from a few minutes ago,

00:22:50.150 --> 00:22:53.600
your voice matters
more than your face.

00:22:53.600 --> 00:22:55.400
You can switch off
video or you can

00:22:55.400 --> 00:22:58.490
go to still images like this
one and give a decent class.

00:22:58.490 --> 00:23:02.450
You cannot lose voice or voice
quality and still have a decent

00:23:02.450 --> 00:23:04.650
class.

00:23:04.650 --> 00:23:10.110
My setup is Samson
microphone and a $25 stand

00:23:10.110 --> 00:23:12.500
for my local music store.

00:23:12.500 --> 00:23:16.070
I got the stand so that the
microphone would be far enough

00:23:16.070 --> 00:23:19.220
away from the keyboard that
it wouldn't be filled with all

00:23:19.220 --> 00:23:21.560
the clicking as I type.

00:23:21.560 --> 00:23:24.080
You can still hear the clicking,
but it's a soft background

00:23:24.080 --> 00:23:26.740
noise rather than thunder.

00:23:26.740 --> 00:23:30.010
Again, try out your rig
with your co-instructor

00:23:30.010 --> 00:23:32.170
or with some randomly
selected student.

00:23:32.170 --> 00:23:34.120
Or if you don't
have anybody else,

00:23:34.120 --> 00:23:37.040
mail me and I will listen to it.

00:23:37.040 --> 00:23:40.380
But getting the mic up
level with your head

00:23:40.380 --> 00:23:44.190
about 30 centimeters
away from your mouth,

00:23:44.190 --> 00:23:47.120
and away from your
keyboard, and that's as much

00:23:47.120 --> 00:23:48.650
as you need to do now.

00:23:48.650 --> 00:23:51.080
If later on you want to go
get spit guards and softballs

00:23:51.080 --> 00:23:54.500
and do the full rig,
absolutely knock yourself out,

00:23:54.500 --> 00:23:58.330
but you don't need to
do that first week.

00:23:58.330 --> 00:24:01.600
What you do need to do is
turn off your notifications.

00:24:05.510 --> 00:24:10.150
You don't want things popping
up on screen constantly telling

00:24:10.150 --> 00:24:14.140
you that you've got mail, that
you've got something on Twitter

00:24:14.140 --> 00:24:15.980
you should be looking at.

00:24:15.980 --> 00:24:17.830
Turn slack off
completely whenever

00:24:17.830 --> 00:24:21.450
I'm teaching, unless I'm
using it to communicate

00:24:21.450 --> 00:24:23.760
with my students.

00:24:23.760 --> 00:24:26.880
Because it's just
a distraction and

00:24:26.880 --> 00:24:30.510
you can ask them to
turn theirs off as well.

00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:33.190
I know that most won't.

00:24:33.190 --> 00:24:37.090
And if you go into a in-person
classroom, stand at the back

00:24:37.090 --> 00:24:39.910
and look over people's
shoulders, at any given time,

00:24:39.910 --> 00:24:42.580
about a third of them are doing
something on their laptop.

00:24:42.580 --> 00:24:45.780
Some of it is course
related some of it isn't.

00:24:45.780 --> 00:24:47.820
Once you're in an
online environment,

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:51.810
there's going to be more of
that, but it's worth asking.

00:24:51.810 --> 00:24:54.900
Again, if you've got a focus
group of about half a dozen

00:24:54.900 --> 00:24:58.730
that you're directing
most of your teaching to,

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:02.300
they're more likely to switch
off distractions and actually

00:25:02.300 --> 00:25:04.520
be engaged because
they're being called on

00:25:04.520 --> 00:25:06.770
with higher frequency.

00:25:06.770 --> 00:25:11.660
So you can get a high quality
experience for a small group,

00:25:11.660 --> 00:25:14.160
and everybody else is going to
do what they would do anyway.

00:25:16.870 --> 00:25:20.495
Now we come to that shared doc
that I've been talking about.

00:25:20.495 --> 00:25:22.370
Excuse me a second, I
need a mouthful of tea.

00:25:27.570 --> 00:25:30.330
Every time I teach
online, I create

00:25:30.330 --> 00:25:33.810
something like a Google Doc
or a HackMD for those of you

00:25:33.810 --> 00:25:35.890
who love markdown.

00:25:35.890 --> 00:25:40.490
And I give edit permission
to the people I'm teaching.

00:25:40.490 --> 00:25:44.060
Whenever I ask a question and
want a response from everybody,

00:25:44.060 --> 00:25:45.750
I now have options.

00:25:45.750 --> 00:25:49.880
I can either tell them
put it in the chat, which

00:25:49.880 --> 00:25:53.300
I will do if I'm looking for a
one word or two word response.

00:25:53.300 --> 00:25:56.090
Or if I'm asking
something a little larger

00:25:56.090 --> 00:25:58.470
and they need to show
me a bit of code,

00:25:58.470 --> 00:26:01.700
write a couple of sentences,
something like that,

00:26:01.700 --> 00:26:04.680
they pasted in to the document.

00:26:04.680 --> 00:26:08.220
I say pasted in
because a lot of people

00:26:08.220 --> 00:26:12.380
don't want you to
see them typing.

00:26:12.380 --> 00:26:15.780
I'm comfortable with that, but
I've been doing this for years.

00:26:15.780 --> 00:26:18.020
What I see a lot
of newcomers do is

00:26:18.020 --> 00:26:19.940
how to separate
text editor where

00:26:19.940 --> 00:26:22.785
they're composing their answer
to question number three,

00:26:22.785 --> 00:26:24.410
and then we'll copy
it and they'll just

00:26:24.410 --> 00:26:26.740
paste it into the Google Doc.

00:26:26.740 --> 00:26:28.380
That's cool.

00:26:28.380 --> 00:26:30.750
As they become more
comfortable, maybe they'll

00:26:30.750 --> 00:26:32.940
do their editing
directly in the doc

00:26:32.940 --> 00:26:34.620
so that others can
see their mistakes

00:26:34.620 --> 00:26:36.060
and see them being corrected.

00:26:36.060 --> 00:26:38.060
That's cool too.

00:26:38.060 --> 00:26:39.980
The important thing
is that it allows

00:26:39.980 --> 00:26:42.020
you to see their
work in real time,

00:26:42.020 --> 00:26:45.410
and that allows them to see one
another's work in real time.

00:26:45.410 --> 00:26:48.170
That dramatically
improves engagement

00:26:48.170 --> 00:26:50.540
and you get a lot of
peer to peer learning.

00:26:50.540 --> 00:26:54.140
If you ask people
to, for example,

00:26:54.140 --> 00:26:57.560
find the height of the
tallest person in Louisiana

00:26:57.560 --> 00:27:02.180
in 1920 using a
dplyr pipeline, there

00:27:02.180 --> 00:27:04.880
are three or four
plausible ways to arrange

00:27:04.880 --> 00:27:07.080
the commands to do that.

00:27:07.080 --> 00:27:09.245
Each person will
have one and then

00:27:09.245 --> 00:27:11.620
they'll be able to glance at
a few others in the document

00:27:11.620 --> 00:27:13.530
to go, oh, that's cool.

00:27:13.530 --> 00:27:17.400
It's exactly like what
we do in a music class

00:27:17.400 --> 00:27:20.370
where I say, OK, Janet
you play this piece.

00:27:20.370 --> 00:27:23.790
OK, now Janine you
play this piece.

00:27:23.790 --> 00:27:27.660
We're used to seeing and hearing
others work in live contexts.

00:27:27.660 --> 00:27:34.110
We can do that online, and this
is probably the biggest change

00:27:34.110 --> 00:27:37.830
in my teaching practice
over the last 10 years

00:27:37.830 --> 00:27:39.990
is trusting learners
to share work

00:27:39.990 --> 00:27:42.000
with each other in real time.

00:27:42.000 --> 00:27:44.140
Couple of caveats for this.

00:27:44.140 --> 00:27:45.780
Number one, if
you're going to do

00:27:45.780 --> 00:27:49.950
it keep that list of half a
dozen names or the entire class

00:27:49.950 --> 00:27:53.720
name depending on how many
are contributing to the doc.

00:27:53.720 --> 00:27:57.110
Paste it into the doc before
they start typing, and do

00:27:57.110 --> 00:28:00.590
that for each question so that
everybody knows where they're

00:28:00.590 --> 00:28:03.020
supposed to start typing.

00:28:03.020 --> 00:28:05.930
If you say just add
this to the doc,

00:28:05.930 --> 00:28:08.240
you'll have two dozen
people putting their cursors

00:28:08.240 --> 00:28:10.580
on top of each other
and colliding with one

00:28:10.580 --> 00:28:12.390
another's work.

00:28:12.390 --> 00:28:14.150
That's not helpful.

00:28:14.150 --> 00:28:17.220
They'll very quickly opt out
because it's frustrating.

00:28:17.220 --> 00:28:20.130
If on the other hand,
I've got a list of names

00:28:20.130 --> 00:28:23.460
with a couple of blank lines
between them in the text file

00:28:23.460 --> 00:28:25.560
and I pasted into the
doc, everybody immediately

00:28:25.560 --> 00:28:29.610
knows where to type and
there's no collisions.

00:28:29.610 --> 00:28:32.400
The second thing has
to do with scope.

00:28:32.400 --> 00:28:36.180
Google doc will comfortably
support 14 or 15, maybe

00:28:36.180 --> 00:28:38.550
16 concurrent typists.

00:28:38.550 --> 00:28:40.350
It won't support 30.

00:28:40.350 --> 00:28:43.482
You'll see a sudden dramatic
slowdown at some threshold.

00:28:43.482 --> 00:28:44.940
And I've never done
the experiments

00:28:44.940 --> 00:28:46.740
to measure where it
is but it's somewhere

00:28:46.740 --> 00:28:49.760
between a dozen and 20.

00:28:49.760 --> 00:28:54.080
Again, you solve this problem
by having a subset of people

00:28:54.080 --> 00:28:57.680
contributing to
the shared document

00:28:57.680 --> 00:28:59.480
and swapping that
out each time you

00:28:59.480 --> 00:29:04.190
take a coffee break, each
time you restart your class.

00:29:04.190 --> 00:29:06.630
Talked about this before,
keep track of who has

00:29:06.630 --> 00:29:09.500
and hasn't spoken to make
sure that the people who

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:13.110
aren't extroverts
are getting air time.

00:29:13.110 --> 00:29:15.870
Doesn't have to be
electronic, just a piece

00:29:15.870 --> 00:29:20.460
of paper and a pencil beside
you putting tick marks in,

00:29:20.460 --> 00:29:22.710
trying to make sure that you
distribute your attention

00:29:22.710 --> 00:29:25.955
fairly so that everybody feels
like they're not just allowed

00:29:25.955 --> 00:29:27.830
in the room, but they're
welcome in the room.

00:29:30.830 --> 00:29:34.220
Ask-fill-call is a
pretty common tactic

00:29:34.220 --> 00:29:37.700
from the days of
broadcast radio.

00:29:37.700 --> 00:29:41.600
If I ask a question,
it's going to take people

00:29:41.600 --> 00:29:43.295
a while to formulate an answer.

00:29:46.040 --> 00:29:50.060
If I'm asking them for a long
answer, three lines of code,

00:29:50.060 --> 00:29:54.020
for example, it's probably
going to take them 15 seconds

00:29:54.020 --> 00:30:03.290
and even a gap of seven
seconds, which you just heard,

00:30:03.290 --> 00:30:05.580
can be really uncomfortable.

00:30:05.580 --> 00:30:08.750
So if I want somebody to
come in with a question,

00:30:08.750 --> 00:30:10.700
I ask the question.

00:30:10.700 --> 00:30:16.050
And then as they're composing
their answer, I fill the air.

00:30:16.050 --> 00:30:17.580
I say something else.

00:30:17.580 --> 00:30:20.155
I elaborate on a previous point.

00:30:20.155 --> 00:30:21.780
They'll probably tune
me out while they

00:30:21.780 --> 00:30:24.420
focus on their answer
so it can't be anything

00:30:24.420 --> 00:30:27.420
too important,
but it's like it's

00:30:27.420 --> 00:30:32.430
making small talk while
somebody is setting the table.

00:30:32.430 --> 00:30:35.460
You want to ask a question, you
want to fill in a little bit

00:30:35.460 --> 00:30:38.310
so that there's not
dead air, and then

00:30:38.310 --> 00:30:42.330
you want to say, OK, Sarah,
now what have you got for us.

00:30:42.330 --> 00:30:46.950
Again, you're doing this to make
up for the lack of social cues.

00:30:46.950 --> 00:30:50.520
You don't have that peripheral
awareness of people's attention

00:30:50.520 --> 00:30:52.620
so you're going to have
to be more directive.

00:30:56.410 --> 00:30:58.870
Another way that you
can take advantage

00:30:58.870 --> 00:31:02.860
of the online teaching is to
ask people for predictions.

00:31:02.860 --> 00:31:06.040
This is particularly important
when you're teaching coding.

00:31:06.040 --> 00:31:08.980
If I'm teaching a
programming class,

00:31:08.980 --> 00:31:11.740
I'm probably sharing my
screen so that you can see

00:31:11.740 --> 00:31:14.500
the code as I'm building it.

00:31:14.500 --> 00:31:16.660
Every time I'm about
to do something,

00:31:16.660 --> 00:31:19.480
I have an opportunity to
say, all right, everybody,

00:31:19.480 --> 00:31:23.140
in the chat, what's going
to happen if I run it now.

00:31:23.140 --> 00:31:28.030
I've just added this line,
how is it going to behave?

00:31:28.030 --> 00:31:30.400
Pedagogically, this
is forcing people

00:31:30.400 --> 00:31:33.160
to pull recent knowledge
back into consciousness

00:31:33.160 --> 00:31:35.740
and use it and reinforce it.

00:31:35.740 --> 00:31:38.430
That will help them
learn and remember.

00:31:38.430 --> 00:31:40.470
From a motivational
point of view,

00:31:40.470 --> 00:31:41.790
it increases their engagement.

00:31:41.790 --> 00:31:45.570
They're constantly being asked
to think and to contribute.

00:31:45.570 --> 00:31:49.050
And from your point of view,
it gives you a very quick

00:31:49.050 --> 00:31:51.000
formative assessment.

00:31:51.000 --> 00:31:52.920
For those who don't
know the term,

00:31:52.920 --> 00:31:55.170
formative assessment
is what you do

00:31:55.170 --> 00:31:58.020
during learning to
find out whether or not

00:31:58.020 --> 00:31:59.670
people are learning.

00:31:59.670 --> 00:32:02.310
It's your real
time feedback loop.

00:32:02.310 --> 00:32:05.370
You cannot trust
people to go, got it,

00:32:05.370 --> 00:32:07.860
I got it because a lot
of people will lie.

00:32:07.860 --> 00:32:10.590
They'll say they have it
when they don't, or they'll

00:32:10.590 --> 00:32:12.840
believe that they do
but they'll be wrong.

00:32:12.840 --> 00:32:16.440
And they're even more
likely to mislead this way

00:32:16.440 --> 00:32:18.750
when you're teaching online.

00:32:18.750 --> 00:32:21.030
If, on the other hand,
I say, all right,

00:32:21.030 --> 00:32:24.810
[INAUDIBLE] what's
this going to do next?

00:32:24.810 --> 00:32:28.910
If they're right, you know
that their understanding.

00:32:28.910 --> 00:32:31.780
If they're wrong, then
you know that you've

00:32:31.780 --> 00:32:34.370
got something to explain
to clarify or to cover

00:32:34.370 --> 00:32:35.367
a second time.

00:32:35.367 --> 00:32:36.950
And you can do it
right then and there

00:32:36.950 --> 00:32:39.940
before their misconception has
a chance to set in concrete.

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:49.070
This is a point where I would
normally pull my class to say,

00:32:49.070 --> 00:32:51.750
how many of you
know breakout rooms.

00:32:51.750 --> 00:32:55.110
For those of you who don't,
if you're using something

00:32:55.110 --> 00:32:59.360
like Zoom, you can go
down and put everybody

00:32:59.360 --> 00:33:01.730
into a small sub conversation.

00:33:01.730 --> 00:33:05.690
You can assign them manually or
you can assign them at random.

00:33:05.690 --> 00:33:08.000
You can vary the
size of the rooms,

00:33:08.000 --> 00:33:09.710
and you can determine
how long they're

00:33:09.710 --> 00:33:12.930
in that side conversation.

00:33:12.930 --> 00:33:16.040
Here's a question, the
answer isn't obvious.

00:33:16.040 --> 00:33:18.320
Everybody go and think
about it and then

00:33:18.320 --> 00:33:21.050
argue with your three
partners and see

00:33:21.050 --> 00:33:23.450
if you can come to a
consensus about what

00:33:23.450 --> 00:33:25.460
this code does or doesn't do.

00:33:25.460 --> 00:33:28.040
3, 2, 1, go.

00:33:28.040 --> 00:33:31.400
You've got a couple of minutes,
and then I pull you back

00:33:31.400 --> 00:33:34.970
and I pull a couple of people.

00:33:34.970 --> 00:33:39.740
This emulates a practice
called peer instruction, which

00:33:39.740 --> 00:33:44.250
is the most effective scalable
teaching practice we know.

00:33:44.250 --> 00:33:46.560
The slide deck has
a link to a video

00:33:46.560 --> 00:33:49.350
from a math class
in India in 2013

00:33:49.350 --> 00:33:51.390
that illustrates the method.

00:33:51.390 --> 00:33:56.690
Short version is I ask a
motivating question, something

00:33:56.690 --> 00:34:01.220
whose answer requires more
than just factual recall.

00:34:01.220 --> 00:34:05.430
You all then go and argue with
each other over the answer,

00:34:05.430 --> 00:34:08.340
and then I pull you back and
give you the explanation.

00:34:08.340 --> 00:34:09.870
Why the argument stage?

00:34:09.870 --> 00:34:11.520
Because that's when
you have to think

00:34:11.520 --> 00:34:15.659
through the inconsistencies
or gaps in your mental model,

00:34:15.659 --> 00:34:18.750
and you've got other people
there who can fill them in.

00:34:18.750 --> 00:34:21.300
And one of the great
discoveries is it doesn't really

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:23.820
matter if anybody in one of
these little discussion groups

00:34:23.820 --> 00:34:24.780
is right at the start.

00:34:24.780 --> 00:34:27.659
What matters is that you
disagree so that you can clear

00:34:27.659 --> 00:34:29.969
up each other's misconceptions.

00:34:29.969 --> 00:34:32.820
Pedagocially, it's a
well-justified method.

00:34:32.820 --> 00:34:36.300
Practically, it also helps
with motivation because instead

00:34:36.300 --> 00:34:40.810
of only teaching to half
a dozen for half an hour,

00:34:40.810 --> 00:34:42.909
you can teach to that
half dozen and then

00:34:42.909 --> 00:34:46.600
say, OK, now everybody is going
into a small discussion group

00:34:46.600 --> 00:34:49.690
and then I'm bringing you
back, and now they have

00:34:49.690 --> 00:34:52.350
to interact with their peers.

00:34:52.350 --> 00:34:56.670
Again, you want people
to be able to opt out.

00:34:56.670 --> 00:34:58.260
They may not be in
a situation where

00:34:58.260 --> 00:35:00.180
they can have a conversation.

00:35:00.180 --> 00:35:03.780
They might be in the living room
muted because the entire family

00:35:03.780 --> 00:35:05.235
is here at the mall.

00:35:05.235 --> 00:35:06.360
You've got to respect that.

00:35:06.360 --> 00:35:09.630
Some people may be uncomfortable
arguing with strangers

00:35:09.630 --> 00:35:11.130
at the outset of a class.

00:35:11.130 --> 00:35:12.270
You respect that.

00:35:12.270 --> 00:35:16.820
But for everyone else, it's
a very effective technique.

00:35:16.820 --> 00:35:22.270
One of the things that we can't
do with any video tool I have

00:35:22.270 --> 00:35:25.850
today is put people
into breakout rooms

00:35:25.850 --> 00:35:29.330
in which they can all screen
share with each other.

00:35:29.330 --> 00:35:33.410
I would really like to be
able to put people into pairs

00:35:33.410 --> 00:35:37.390
and have them screen share
so that they can co-program.

00:35:37.390 --> 00:35:39.580
The technology will
get there eventually,

00:35:39.580 --> 00:35:41.740
but something like
that or a shared

00:35:41.740 --> 00:35:46.270
whiteboard in the breakout
room would probably help a lot.

00:35:46.270 --> 00:35:48.080
We just have to wait.

00:35:48.080 --> 00:35:50.330
And since I mentioned
whiteboards,

00:35:50.330 --> 00:35:53.720
Zoom provides polls so that
you can ask people questions

00:35:53.720 --> 00:35:56.390
in real time and do a survey.

00:35:56.390 --> 00:35:58.970
It also provides
a whiteboard don't

00:35:58.970 --> 00:36:01.790
try to use it yet,
become comfortable

00:36:01.790 --> 00:36:03.530
with the online
teaching, sort out

00:36:03.530 --> 00:36:05.488
some of your other
problems, and then start

00:36:05.488 --> 00:36:07.280
to try to take advantage
of all the science

00:36:07.280 --> 00:36:09.410
fiction you've been handed.

00:36:09.410 --> 00:36:11.080
You don't need it today.

00:36:11.080 --> 00:36:12.330
Take advantage of it tomorrow.

00:36:17.350 --> 00:36:20.320
The quote here
comes from my wife

00:36:20.320 --> 00:36:22.780
who once explained to me that
the basic unit of teaching

00:36:22.780 --> 00:36:24.550
is the bladder.

00:36:24.550 --> 00:36:26.560
And I said, I've never
thought of it that way.

00:36:26.560 --> 00:36:29.610
And she said, you've
never been pregnant.

00:36:29.610 --> 00:36:32.180
You need to take a
break every hour.

00:36:32.180 --> 00:36:34.370
Your back's going to
get sore, your voice

00:36:34.370 --> 00:36:37.040
is going to get hoarse,
your learners are probably

00:36:37.040 --> 00:36:40.070
in situations that are no
more comfortable than yours

00:36:40.070 --> 00:36:42.200
and quite probably less.

00:36:42.200 --> 00:36:45.320
And even when they're
comfortable, it's a long day

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:47.330
and it's a lot of coffee.

00:36:47.330 --> 00:36:52.310
Make sure that you set yourself
a timer, I do it on my phone,

00:36:52.310 --> 00:36:55.990
and have it go
off at 45 minutes.

00:36:55.990 --> 00:36:56.500
I found

00:36:56.500 --> 00:36:58.900
That when I was setting
timers for an hour

00:36:58.900 --> 00:37:01.420
because I always
drift a little over,

00:37:01.420 --> 00:37:05.500
we were only taking breaks
about every 75 rate minutes.

00:37:05.500 --> 00:37:08.140
If I set the timer
for 45 minutes,

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:10.795
I'm pretty much guaranteed
a break every hour.

00:37:10.795 --> 00:37:13.420
Whenever I set up for an hour I
always go over 5 or 10 minutes.

00:37:17.130 --> 00:37:21.350
And to wrap up, before we
start taking questions,

00:37:21.350 --> 00:37:24.290
I want you all to know
that nobody knows anything

00:37:24.290 --> 00:37:26.990
right now.

00:37:26.990 --> 00:37:31.700
We've been teaching online
in relatively small numbers,

00:37:31.700 --> 00:37:35.480
in relatively constrained
technological environments

00:37:35.480 --> 00:37:41.020
for a couple of
decades, maybe three.

00:37:41.020 --> 00:37:45.710
And on the one hand, that sounds
like a lot, but on the other,

00:37:45.710 --> 00:37:50.350
we are about to invent more
different ways to teach online

00:37:50.350 --> 00:37:54.010
in the next 12 months than
we know in total today

00:37:54.010 --> 00:37:58.847
because we're about to do
a whole lot of experiments.

00:37:58.847 --> 00:38:00.430
You're going to suck
at this at first,

00:38:00.430 --> 00:38:04.080
but that's a necessary first
step towards being good at it.

00:38:04.080 --> 00:38:06.960
Don't beat yourself up.

00:38:06.960 --> 00:38:09.510
And don't be afraid
to experiment.

00:38:09.510 --> 00:38:12.510
Don't be afraid to
try things, and then

00:38:12.510 --> 00:38:15.030
to say all right,
that didn't work.

00:38:15.030 --> 00:38:17.190
I think everybody
understands that these

00:38:17.190 --> 00:38:19.530
are unusual circumstances.

00:38:19.530 --> 00:38:22.470
I think everybody,
I hope, is going

00:38:22.470 --> 00:38:26.160
to give people a
little bit of slack,

00:38:26.160 --> 00:38:29.460
give them a little bit
of allowance because

00:38:29.460 --> 00:38:31.980
of the unusual circumstances.

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:37.770
So there's never been better
time to try new things.

00:38:37.770 --> 00:38:41.020
Your audience will never
be more understanding.

00:38:41.020 --> 00:38:43.810
And as I said at the
outset, we're here to help.

00:38:43.810 --> 00:38:46.690
If there's anything we can do,
if there's any questions you

00:38:46.690 --> 00:38:51.310
have, throw them at us.

00:38:51.310 --> 00:38:55.280
Last thing before we
dive into the close,

00:38:55.280 --> 00:38:57.590
if you've got time
to do any reading,

00:38:57.590 --> 00:39:02.670
I highly recommend this book
by Brookfield and Preskill.

00:39:02.670 --> 00:39:07.360
I've been talking about
things with people,

00:39:07.360 --> 00:39:10.420
in large groups
since I was about 60.

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:13.330
Of the 50 different
techniques that catalog

00:39:13.330 --> 00:39:16.870
in this book for having
a productive discussion,

00:39:16.870 --> 00:39:20.320
I had only ever seen 15,
and I've only ever seen

00:39:20.320 --> 00:39:22.750
five or six implemented online.

00:39:22.750 --> 00:39:25.630
This is a catalog of
ways to get people

00:39:25.630 --> 00:39:31.210
to talk to one another and share
information and make decisions.

00:39:31.210 --> 00:39:34.510
A lot of them are directly
applicable to teaching.

00:39:34.510 --> 00:39:37.810
And even if you don't
have direct customized

00:39:37.810 --> 00:39:40.690
technical support, they're
all social conventions

00:39:40.690 --> 00:39:44.860
that are fairly easy to adopt
in an online environment.

00:39:44.860 --> 00:39:47.470
Just to pick one
example, suppose

00:39:47.470 --> 00:39:50.640
I've got half a dozen
people that I'm focusing on.

00:39:50.640 --> 00:39:52.960
All right, I want all of
you to put your answers

00:39:52.960 --> 00:39:55.120
to this question
into the Google Doc.

00:39:55.120 --> 00:39:57.220
OK, now that we've
got those, I want

00:39:57.220 --> 00:40:01.210
you each to shift down one spot,
bottom person comes to the top,

00:40:01.210 --> 00:40:04.900
and comment on the
answer from the person

00:40:04.900 --> 00:40:06.410
that you're now looking at.

00:40:06.410 --> 00:40:09.310
OK, now shift down one
more, so now you're

00:40:09.310 --> 00:40:13.830
looking at an answer and a
previous set of comments.

00:40:13.830 --> 00:40:16.110
People will learn so
much from each other,

00:40:16.110 --> 00:40:18.240
and the peer to peer
knowledge transfer

00:40:18.240 --> 00:40:21.330
will make up for so much of
what you're losing by not

00:40:21.330 --> 00:40:23.143
having to physically present.

00:40:23.143 --> 00:40:25.060
And that's just one idea
in a book full of 50.

00:40:30.570 --> 00:40:33.240
Here are some links that I
found useful preparing this.

00:40:33.240 --> 00:40:36.570
Jason Bell, in Australia, who I
believe is here with us today,

00:40:36.570 --> 00:40:39.540
has a good video on what
it's like to teach software

00:40:39.540 --> 00:40:41.880
carpentry workshops remotely.

00:40:41.880 --> 00:40:43.020
He's in Queensland.

00:40:43.020 --> 00:40:44.580
A lot of his learners
are scattered

00:40:44.580 --> 00:40:47.160
so he's had to figure
out over the years

00:40:47.160 --> 00:40:50.820
how you teach programming when
you are hundreds of kilometers

00:40:50.820 --> 00:40:53.040
away from all of your
learners, and they're

00:40:53.040 --> 00:40:55.900
hundreds of kilometers
away from each other.

00:40:55.900 --> 00:40:58.420
Elizabeth Wickes, I
cited a couple of times,

00:40:58.420 --> 00:41:01.300
has a really good set
of tips for teaching

00:41:01.300 --> 00:41:04.650
tech subjects online.

00:41:04.650 --> 00:41:06.840
Does a growing number of
comments in the RStudio

00:41:06.840 --> 00:41:08.490
community teaching
discussion, you're

00:41:08.490 --> 00:41:11.160
all welcome to join in their.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:14.280
I'm a big fan of the piece
that Laura Czerniewicz wrote

00:41:14.280 --> 00:41:17.400
in South Africa, what they
learned from going online

00:41:17.400 --> 00:41:20.190
in Cape Town on short notice.

00:41:20.190 --> 00:41:24.030
That link there
includes a sub link

00:41:24.030 --> 00:41:26.610
to a document about
how to accommodate

00:41:26.610 --> 00:41:30.450
wildly different technical
levels among your students.

00:41:30.450 --> 00:41:32.790
They'll have differential
access to devices

00:41:32.790 --> 00:41:35.430
to bandwidth, whole
bunch of things,

00:41:35.430 --> 00:41:38.850
they've got a lot of experience
about how you accommodate that.

00:41:38.850 --> 00:41:40.710
So please follow that one up.

00:41:40.710 --> 00:41:42.900
And finally, just today
there was another blog post

00:41:42.900 --> 00:41:45.300
from the Carpentries,
they're collecting their tips

00:41:45.300 --> 00:41:47.040
for teaching online,
and again, there

00:41:47.040 --> 00:41:50.070
are links to other resources.

00:41:50.070 --> 00:41:53.970
So with all of that done, I
am hoping that some people

00:41:53.970 --> 00:41:55.800
will have questions.

00:41:55.800 --> 00:41:59.040
So Leon Justin is saying
that, calling people by name

00:41:59.040 --> 00:42:01.500
is referred to as
harpooning and discourage.

00:42:01.500 --> 00:42:03.630
Yes, like any
teaching technique,

00:42:03.630 --> 00:42:05.820
it can be used badly.

00:42:05.820 --> 00:42:07.610
You risk lower tier
and introvert students

00:42:07.610 --> 00:42:09.360
leaving the teaching
platform because they

00:42:09.360 --> 00:42:12.010
don't want to be called out.

00:42:12.010 --> 00:42:16.680
I respect that normally,
you can detect that

00:42:16.680 --> 00:42:20.940
or you can ask them in advance
to message you if they are

00:42:20.940 --> 00:42:23.970
uncomfortable being called on.

00:42:23.970 --> 00:42:26.750
Just have them tell
you, all right,

00:42:26.750 --> 00:42:29.360
and respect whatever
it is they do.

00:42:29.360 --> 00:42:32.720
Elisa Dillman is looking for
a list of accessibility tools.

00:42:32.720 --> 00:42:34.610
Yes, we've got a
blog post hopefully

00:42:34.610 --> 00:42:38.110
up sometime next week.

00:42:38.110 --> 00:42:42.280
Does a cardioid mic help
reduce the keyboard noise?

00:42:42.280 --> 00:42:44.200
It can.

00:42:44.200 --> 00:42:45.630
This Samson isn't a cardioid.

00:42:45.630 --> 00:42:47.587
I've just got it
by trial and error.

00:42:47.587 --> 00:42:50.170
I've got to tilt it at an angle
where it seems to just pick up

00:42:50.170 --> 00:42:51.790
my voice and not the keyboard.

00:42:51.790 --> 00:42:54.310
For those who don't know
the term a cardioid mic,

00:42:54.310 --> 00:42:58.040
picks up sound on one side or
the other, but not in between.

00:42:58.040 --> 00:43:00.640
It's a directional
microphone intended primarily

00:43:00.640 --> 00:43:03.220
for interviews where
you've got two people

00:43:03.220 --> 00:43:05.260
on opposite sides of a mic.

00:43:05.260 --> 00:43:07.007
One of the reason
I got the Samson

00:43:07.007 --> 00:43:08.590
is that it's a cheap
enough microphone

00:43:08.590 --> 00:43:11.360
that if I lose it while
traveling, I'm not heartbroken.

00:43:11.360 --> 00:43:14.110
A good cardioid mic is
going to be more expensive.

00:43:17.160 --> 00:43:19.860
Laura [INAUDIBLE] feel lost
reading all the feedback

00:43:19.860 --> 00:43:22.280
from the students
in a shared doc.

00:43:22.280 --> 00:43:24.030
Do you have any tips
to read the feedback?

00:43:24.030 --> 00:43:28.780
This is part of what your
co-instructor can do for you.

00:43:28.780 --> 00:43:32.680
Normally when I'm teaching,
I will call a one or two,

00:43:32.680 --> 00:43:34.540
but I've got somebody
else there just

00:43:34.540 --> 00:43:37.090
as I've had Robert and Curtis
going through your questions

00:43:37.090 --> 00:43:40.120
and flagging them.

00:43:40.120 --> 00:43:43.330
So that by the time I
get there, somebody else

00:43:43.330 --> 00:43:47.490
has already done the
first pass of filtering.

00:43:47.490 --> 00:43:50.180
Ryan Davis, for smaller
class doing quick intros

00:43:50.180 --> 00:43:50.902
at the start.

00:43:50.902 --> 00:43:52.360
Absolutely, if
you've got the time,

00:43:52.360 --> 00:43:55.310
if you're comfortable doing it.

00:43:55.310 --> 00:43:57.230
And then Kelsey
[INAUDIBLE] do you

00:43:57.230 --> 00:44:01.220
have a tactic for keeping
track of participant responses?

00:44:01.220 --> 00:44:04.460
When they may have strange
and recognizable names,

00:44:04.460 --> 00:44:10.530
yes, I sometimes
now have learners

00:44:10.530 --> 00:44:14.570
whose name field is an emoji.

00:44:14.570 --> 00:44:17.145
In those cases, I
will ask people,

00:44:17.145 --> 00:44:19.520
can you please change it for
the duration of the session,

00:44:19.520 --> 00:44:22.260
or just let me know
how to address you.

00:44:22.260 --> 00:44:25.410
I find that asking people how
do you want to be addressed also

00:44:25.410 --> 00:44:30.390
helps with internationalization
of names and pronouns.

00:44:30.390 --> 00:44:34.050
I'm quite often not sure
which of the several parts

00:44:34.050 --> 00:44:35.940
of someone's name
the most useful--

00:44:35.940 --> 00:44:38.010
most used to being called
by particularly when

00:44:38.010 --> 00:44:40.200
I get into East Asian
and South Asian cultures

00:44:40.200 --> 00:44:42.120
that I'm not familiar with.

00:44:42.120 --> 00:44:45.585
And it's always a good idea
to ask people what pronoun

00:44:45.585 --> 00:44:46.710
they would like you to use.

00:44:49.115 --> 00:44:50.990
Students don't turn on
the video because they

00:44:50.990 --> 00:44:53.210
don't feel comfortable.

00:44:53.210 --> 00:44:55.460
This one's coming
from Gaby Sandoval.

00:44:55.460 --> 00:44:56.870
In many cases,
they don't turn it

00:44:56.870 --> 00:45:00.430
on because they're
busy with other things.

00:45:00.430 --> 00:45:02.500
They don't want you to
see that they are also

00:45:02.500 --> 00:45:04.360
doing their calculus
homework while they're

00:45:04.360 --> 00:45:06.610
supposed to be in your class.

00:45:06.610 --> 00:45:09.250
I ask them I try to create an
environment in which they're

00:45:09.250 --> 00:45:13.300
comfortable being seen focusing
on a small number at a time

00:45:13.300 --> 00:45:15.470
does seem to help with this.

00:45:15.470 --> 00:45:18.870
I think it's partly
a matter of trust.

00:45:18.870 --> 00:45:21.540
I'm afraid I don't have
anything more specific than that

00:45:21.540 --> 00:45:23.070
that I can recommend.

00:45:23.070 --> 00:45:24.600
I think people are
going to become

00:45:24.600 --> 00:45:26.460
more comfortable
in a hurry as we

00:45:26.460 --> 00:45:30.840
all start doing a lot
more online in a hurry.

00:45:30.840 --> 00:45:33.840
Lyn Williams, how do you work
with Google Docs and HackMD

00:45:33.840 --> 00:45:35.200
when it's new to your class?

00:45:35.200 --> 00:45:37.290
And they've said it's
use is overwhelming.

00:45:37.290 --> 00:45:38.880
Really good question.

00:45:38.880 --> 00:45:41.250
I don't use it on day one.

00:45:41.250 --> 00:45:43.170
I will sometimes for
instructor training

00:45:43.170 --> 00:45:46.800
because we've only got
a one, but in a lot

00:45:46.800 --> 00:45:48.780
of other circumstances,
I'm going

00:45:48.780 --> 00:45:50.970
to introduce these
practices to people

00:45:50.970 --> 00:45:54.780
one at a time over the
course of several lessons.

00:45:54.780 --> 00:45:58.320
First day, just get them used
to using Zoom and taking turns

00:45:58.320 --> 00:46:01.650
speaking because that's
the most important thing.

00:46:01.650 --> 00:46:05.100
Second class with them,
get them used to the idea

00:46:05.100 --> 00:46:07.470
that when I ask a
question, they can all

00:46:07.470 --> 00:46:10.560
give short responses in chat.

00:46:10.560 --> 00:46:12.360
It's not archived, it'll fly.

00:46:12.360 --> 00:46:15.240
By I probably don't
even read all of them

00:46:15.240 --> 00:46:17.070
but I'm getting used
to them getting them

00:46:17.070 --> 00:46:19.140
used to contributing.

00:46:19.140 --> 00:46:22.050
Things like a
focus group, things

00:46:22.050 --> 00:46:23.790
like turning on their
own video, things

00:46:23.790 --> 00:46:29.320
like using the Google Doc,
lesson two, lesson three.

00:46:29.320 --> 00:46:31.480
Coming down a bit,
how do you make

00:46:31.480 --> 00:46:33.400
sure each student
is paying attention?

00:46:33.400 --> 00:46:35.050
You can't.

00:46:35.050 --> 00:46:36.070
This is from Mathias.

00:46:36.070 --> 00:46:38.440
We can't do it in person either.

00:46:38.440 --> 00:46:41.410
I remember very clearly being in
an electrical engineering class

00:46:41.410 --> 00:46:43.350
watching a colleague teach.

00:46:43.350 --> 00:46:45.190
And since I was
standing at the back

00:46:45.190 --> 00:46:47.470
and looking down
these rows of seats,

00:46:47.470 --> 00:46:49.540
I could see that almost
a third of the students

00:46:49.540 --> 00:46:53.187
were playing poker online
during the lecture.

00:46:53.187 --> 00:46:54.770
We're not going to
do better than that

00:46:54.770 --> 00:46:56.020
when we shift them all online.

00:46:59.260 --> 00:47:03.010
What we can do is the best we
can with the tools we have.

00:47:06.010 --> 00:47:10.510
Karina Marks, whereby.com Skype
for business, Microsoft Teams.

00:47:10.510 --> 00:47:13.300
I like whereby.com a lot.

00:47:13.300 --> 00:47:15.110
For those of you
who haven't seen it,

00:47:15.110 --> 00:47:17.980
it uses modern web
standards like webRTC.

00:47:17.980 --> 00:47:19.870
It's a beautiful little tool.

00:47:19.870 --> 00:47:22.540
I think Skype for Business is
a really good advertisement

00:47:22.540 --> 00:47:23.920
for Zoom.

00:47:23.920 --> 00:47:25.900
I was on a Skype
call Tuesday night

00:47:25.900 --> 00:47:28.810
for the first time in ages
with my guitar teacher

00:47:28.810 --> 00:47:31.060
and we're going to
try Zoom next time.

00:47:31.060 --> 00:47:33.923
I haven't used Microsoft Teams.

00:47:33.923 --> 00:47:36.340
I'd be interested in other
people's experience of using it

00:47:36.340 --> 00:47:39.250
as a teaching platform.

00:47:39.250 --> 00:47:42.130
Is there a way to help teachers
how to Zoom if we never have?

00:47:42.130 --> 00:47:43.790
From Michelle Connor.

00:47:43.790 --> 00:47:47.380
OK, if you mail me, I'm
greg.wilson@rstudio.com, we'll

00:47:47.380 --> 00:47:49.393
set up a session, we'll
show you what we know.

00:47:52.111 --> 00:47:54.190
Other recommended
platforms for videocalls

00:47:54.190 --> 00:47:57.870
calls from [INAUDIBLE] basis.

00:47:57.870 --> 00:48:01.080
I'm in Toronto, Canada,
I know what works here.

00:48:01.080 --> 00:48:02.820
I would be very
grateful from comments

00:48:02.820 --> 00:48:06.880
from other people about what
has worked where they are.

00:48:06.880 --> 00:48:09.090
I don't know what a
good videoconferencing

00:48:09.090 --> 00:48:13.270
solution is in Argentina or
Malaysia or South Africa.

00:48:13.270 --> 00:48:15.270
I don't want to give you
misleading information,

00:48:15.270 --> 00:48:18.240
but please throw it into the
RStudio community channel,

00:48:18.240 --> 00:48:20.310
or throw it to me
and I'll share it.

00:48:20.310 --> 00:48:23.140
Why is dead air so bad?

00:48:23.140 --> 00:48:26.350
A couple of moments of quiet
time after hearing a question

00:48:26.350 --> 00:48:29.350
is fine, but a couple
of moments really

00:48:29.350 --> 00:48:31.540
does mean a couple of moments.

00:48:31.540 --> 00:48:38.560
People will become
very uncomfortable

00:48:38.560 --> 00:48:43.040
with a silence like the
one I just did, right?

00:48:43.040 --> 00:48:45.590
If they've got video and can
see that you're still there

00:48:45.590 --> 00:48:48.080
that reduces it a bit.

00:48:48.080 --> 00:48:51.080
But it is still uncomfortable
to have a long pause.

00:48:54.933 --> 00:48:56.600
And Jeremiah is asking,
these are great,

00:48:56.600 --> 00:48:59.720
but many ideas relate to
live or synchronous teaching.

00:48:59.720 --> 00:49:01.970
With my students
scattered, this won't work.

00:49:01.970 --> 00:49:03.800
Any advice on
making asynchronous

00:49:03.800 --> 00:49:05.810
teaching compelling?

00:49:05.810 --> 00:49:08.602
That one's actually
easier to do online

00:49:08.602 --> 00:49:10.310
because you're already
doing a lot of it.

00:49:10.310 --> 00:49:13.220
You're probably already
publishing your slides.

00:49:13.220 --> 00:49:15.170
You're probably
already using some sort

00:49:15.170 --> 00:49:17.030
of homework submission system.

00:49:17.030 --> 00:49:20.270
You probably, hopefully, already
have some sort of discussion

00:49:20.270 --> 00:49:24.170
forum or board or mailing
list set up for your class.

00:49:24.170 --> 00:49:26.810
That stuff won't change.

00:49:26.810 --> 00:49:28.550
If your administration
is pushing

00:49:28.550 --> 00:49:33.730
you to record and publish
videos on short notice,

00:49:33.730 --> 00:49:34.870
please push back.

00:49:34.870 --> 00:49:36.760
It's a lot of work.

00:49:36.760 --> 00:49:38.620
If you're worried
about burning it,

00:49:38.620 --> 00:49:41.440
particularly now that you're
juggling everything else,

00:49:41.440 --> 00:49:43.780
that's going to be the
one that breaks you.

00:49:43.780 --> 00:49:47.230
If you're already
doing async teaching

00:49:47.230 --> 00:49:50.750
with text and images
and static documents,

00:49:50.750 --> 00:49:53.590
that's the way you want to run.

00:49:53.590 --> 00:49:56.250
Leon Jessen, I could
see who is online

00:49:56.250 --> 00:49:58.050
and during the four-hour
class I lost some

00:49:58.050 --> 00:49:59.730
of the lower-tier students.

00:49:59.730 --> 00:50:03.790
So Leon, I would ask
did they drop out

00:50:03.790 --> 00:50:05.650
because it was a
four-hour class?

00:50:05.650 --> 00:50:09.290
Four hours is a long
time in one chunk.

00:50:09.290 --> 00:50:13.210
I mean, human attention is 45
to 90 minutes without a reset.

00:50:13.210 --> 00:50:16.150
I think if you're putting
people into a four-hour class,

00:50:16.150 --> 00:50:19.420
you're going to lose people
for a whole bunch of reasons.

00:50:19.420 --> 00:50:21.820
I couldn't do it these
days because of my back.

00:50:21.820 --> 00:50:25.990
I simply can't sit-in a
chair for four hours at a go.

00:50:25.990 --> 00:50:29.230
The second question is would
be, when you say lower-tier,

00:50:29.230 --> 00:50:31.480
you mean people who
are less advanced,

00:50:31.480 --> 00:50:33.550
people who have
less technical skill

00:50:33.550 --> 00:50:36.520
or have a less solid
grasp of the material.

00:50:36.520 --> 00:50:38.590
In which case, they
were checking out

00:50:38.590 --> 00:50:40.810
in your in-person class as well.

00:50:40.810 --> 00:50:44.500
It just wasn't as obvious,
they were physically present

00:50:44.500 --> 00:50:47.900
but mentally, they had checked
out and now you're seeing it.

00:50:47.900 --> 00:50:51.370
And one of the things that we
get from people logging out

00:50:51.370 --> 00:50:54.783
of Zoom logging
on video calls is

00:50:54.783 --> 00:50:56.950
that we can actually see
who's checking out in a way

00:50:56.950 --> 00:50:57.950
that we couldn't before.

00:50:57.950 --> 00:51:01.850
It's not creating the problem
necessarily, it's revealing it.

00:51:01.850 --> 00:51:03.820
And that may sound like
I'm making an excuse

00:51:03.820 --> 00:51:05.770
or trying to explain it
away, the honest truth

00:51:05.770 --> 00:51:10.744
is I don't know and I don't
know of any research on this.

00:51:10.744 --> 00:51:13.100
Amato Deko, do you
have any tips to notice

00:51:13.100 --> 00:51:17.180
quickly when you're losing
someone during a training?

00:51:17.180 --> 00:51:19.760
Pair them up, put them in
groups as quickly as possible

00:51:19.760 --> 00:51:21.370
so that their peers
can support them.

00:51:21.370 --> 00:51:24.290
Breakout rooms, pair
wise discussions,

00:51:24.290 --> 00:51:27.380
having them review each other's
work in a shared document,

00:51:27.380 --> 00:51:28.820
things like that.

00:51:28.820 --> 00:51:32.000
You can't give individual
attention to a dozen people

00:51:32.000 --> 00:51:34.460
at once, never
mind a class of 50.

00:51:34.460 --> 00:51:37.400
They can give attention
to each other.

00:51:37.400 --> 00:51:39.530
The one thing that
you have to monitor

00:51:39.530 --> 00:51:42.250
is that they're
not being trolls.

00:51:42.250 --> 00:51:46.630
Provided attendance is
named, not anonymous.

00:51:46.630 --> 00:51:49.100
You've got some sort
of control over that.

00:51:49.100 --> 00:51:51.340
But if you have 50
people anonymously

00:51:51.340 --> 00:51:53.740
in a classroom giving
each other feedback,

00:51:53.740 --> 00:51:55.540
somebody is going to
be rude to somebody

00:51:55.540 --> 00:51:57.800
and then you've
lost the learning.

00:51:57.800 --> 00:52:02.460
So I strongly prefer that
people identify themselves.

00:52:02.460 --> 00:52:04.400
And I think since
most of us are working

00:52:04.400 --> 00:52:07.280
in formal educational
contexts, either university

00:52:07.280 --> 00:52:09.680
classroom or a paid
training event,

00:52:09.680 --> 00:52:13.468
it's OK to ask for real names.

00:52:13.468 --> 00:52:15.760
Jeffrey Thompson, we're doing
asynchronous instruction,

00:52:15.760 --> 00:52:18.490
recording short videos
of students watched them

00:52:18.490 --> 00:52:20.800
and then holding
virtual office hours.

00:52:20.800 --> 00:52:21.370
Great.

00:52:21.370 --> 00:52:25.180
This is the equivalent
of a flipped classroom.

00:52:25.180 --> 00:52:28.850
Flipped classroom is one where
the students learn the material

00:52:28.850 --> 00:52:32.180
out of class and do the homework
in class so that when they're

00:52:32.180 --> 00:52:34.280
actually trying to
apply the knowledge,

00:52:34.280 --> 00:52:36.740
you're right there with them
when they need you most.

00:52:36.740 --> 00:52:43.080
Have them work through problems
in that shared session.

00:52:43.080 --> 00:52:45.390
Ask one of them to
share their screen

00:52:45.390 --> 00:52:49.090
and actually saw the
small programming problem.

00:52:49.090 --> 00:52:51.430
Or if they've got a
tablet in a fingertip

00:52:51.430 --> 00:52:54.010
to work out an equation
there in front of people.

00:52:54.010 --> 00:52:55.510
You would bring
somebody up and have

00:52:55.510 --> 00:52:58.457
them to work on a whiteboard
in an in-person class.

00:52:58.457 --> 00:53:00.040
You would have
somebody play a passage

00:53:00.040 --> 00:53:02.320
on a saxophone in
the band class.

00:53:02.320 --> 00:53:05.290
You can do that to
improve engagement,

00:53:05.290 --> 00:53:08.320
and to take full advantage
of the interactivity

00:53:08.320 --> 00:53:10.930
of those office hours.

00:53:10.930 --> 00:53:11.800
Skipping down again.

00:53:14.930 --> 00:53:18.700
Tips for engaging when there
are many, many students?

00:53:18.700 --> 00:53:21.370
All right, something I
should have emphasized more

00:53:21.370 --> 00:53:22.990
during the presentation.

00:53:22.990 --> 00:53:27.280
You are not going to be able
to do better in the next three

00:53:27.280 --> 00:53:29.530
months shifting
online than you're

00:53:29.530 --> 00:53:33.140
doing right now in person.

00:53:33.140 --> 00:53:35.960
One person lecturing
to a class of 1,000

00:53:35.960 --> 00:53:38.490
is a poor learning experience.

00:53:38.490 --> 00:53:40.790
It doesn't matter if
it's online or not.

00:53:40.790 --> 00:53:45.300
Peer instruction goes a long
way towards addressing them.

00:53:45.300 --> 00:53:47.400
Getting them talking to
each other in small groups

00:53:47.400 --> 00:53:50.040
periodically to clear up
each other's misconceptions

00:53:50.040 --> 00:53:53.080
is a proven scalable
approach to this.

00:53:53.080 --> 00:53:55.890
But if you're trying to take
broadcast instruction where

00:53:55.890 --> 00:53:58.950
you're the one delivering
information to all of them

00:53:58.950 --> 00:54:02.070
in an in-person class, a
lot of them aren't engaged

00:54:02.070 --> 00:54:03.510
and a lot of them are lost.

00:54:03.510 --> 00:54:05.160
You just don't realize it.

00:54:05.160 --> 00:54:08.580
So we're not going to be
able to do better right away.

00:54:08.580 --> 00:54:14.020
My goal for the next 90 days
is just not to do worse.

00:54:14.020 --> 00:54:15.880
Coming down to a
couple of others

00:54:15.880 --> 00:54:18.490
because we're
almost out of time.

00:54:18.490 --> 00:54:21.140
Excuse me.

00:54:21.140 --> 00:54:25.100
Some more questions
about async teaching.

00:54:25.100 --> 00:54:26.750
Can you talk about
adding captions

00:54:26.750 --> 00:54:30.170
to live-streamed
content on Zoom?

00:54:30.170 --> 00:54:31.880
Absolutely.

00:54:31.880 --> 00:54:34.670
I had speaker's notes
to the slide deck

00:54:34.670 --> 00:54:39.470
rather it than captioning
the live-streamed content

00:54:39.470 --> 00:54:43.290
because I find that's
easier for people to search.

00:54:43.290 --> 00:54:46.680
I don't have a lot of
experience with adding captions

00:54:46.680 --> 00:54:48.690
to Livestream content.

00:54:48.690 --> 00:54:51.090
If you can throw that
question to me by email,

00:54:51.090 --> 00:54:52.650
I'll go and find
somebody who's done

00:54:52.650 --> 00:54:56.050
this more than I have and we
will come back and give you

00:54:56.050 --> 00:54:58.530
an answer.

00:54:58.530 --> 00:55:00.810
Lisa Johnson, any
advice for sticky notes

00:55:00.810 --> 00:55:04.680
substitutions for participants
to give feedback on the pace?

00:55:04.680 --> 00:55:07.828
For those of you who don't
know a trick pioneered

00:55:07.828 --> 00:55:09.870
in the Carpentries workshops
is to give everybody

00:55:09.870 --> 00:55:11.850
a green sticky and a red sticky.

00:55:11.850 --> 00:55:13.740
Green sticky means
things are going OK.

00:55:13.740 --> 00:55:17.720
Red sticky means I've got
a problem, I need help.

00:55:17.720 --> 00:55:22.860
In Zoom, people can set an icon
thumbs up, thumbs down, how am

00:55:22.860 --> 00:55:24.870
I feeling about this right now.

00:55:24.870 --> 00:55:26.640
It's kind of hard for
you as the teacher

00:55:26.640 --> 00:55:30.420
to monitor those so that's
something your co-pilot can do.

00:55:30.420 --> 00:55:34.020
I wish there were better
controls or something

00:55:34.020 --> 00:55:36.750
a little more
adaptive to indicate,

00:55:36.750 --> 00:55:39.690
but you can use thumbs
up, thumbs down in Zoom.

00:55:39.690 --> 00:55:43.800
I don't know if this is
similar capability in GoToMe--

00:55:43.800 --> 00:55:46.140
GoToWebinar rather.

00:55:46.140 --> 00:55:47.625
Coming down a little further.

00:55:50.460 --> 00:55:53.230
Software for online teaching.

00:55:53.230 --> 00:55:56.310
Take a video conferencing
tool, pick a wiki tool

00:55:56.310 --> 00:55:58.560
or use Google Docs.

00:55:58.560 --> 00:56:00.620
Picks something
for real time chat,

00:56:00.620 --> 00:56:03.650
it can be Slack
or anything else.

00:56:03.650 --> 00:56:07.430
I don't recommend any of
the custom-integrated tools.

00:56:07.430 --> 00:56:09.770
Most learning management
systems are actually

00:56:09.770 --> 00:56:11.553
grading management systems.

00:56:11.553 --> 00:56:13.220
They don't really
help with the learning

00:56:13.220 --> 00:56:16.050
and the interactive experience.

00:56:16.050 --> 00:56:19.520
Lisa Johnson, you
have audio tips--

00:56:19.520 --> 00:56:21.860
to tips for audio issues
where more than one student

00:56:21.860 --> 00:56:24.050
has something to say
at the same time.

00:56:24.050 --> 00:56:26.480
Mute everybody and then
you invite somebody

00:56:26.480 --> 00:56:28.250
into the conversation.

00:56:28.250 --> 00:56:30.800
Parliamentary rules, nobody
speaks unless the speaker

00:56:30.800 --> 00:56:32.240
calls on them.

00:56:32.240 --> 00:56:34.640
When you're doing that,
your job as the teacher

00:56:34.640 --> 00:56:37.460
becomes that as more
of a traffic director.

00:56:37.460 --> 00:56:41.900
You're deciding who
speaks next and why.

00:56:41.900 --> 00:56:44.780
It doesn't take very long
for people to get used to it.

00:56:44.780 --> 00:56:47.285
And if you're proactive
about meeting everybody,

00:56:47.285 --> 00:56:49.160
they'll pretty quickly
learn that they're not

00:56:49.160 --> 00:56:51.510
going to be allowed
to break that rule.

00:56:51.510 --> 00:56:54.300
How do you deal with trolls?

00:56:54.300 --> 00:56:56.700
Sadir Shukla.

00:56:56.700 --> 00:56:59.070
Really important question,
you deal with them

00:56:59.070 --> 00:57:02.970
the same way that you would
in an in-person class.

00:57:02.970 --> 00:57:06.120
You start by reminding everybody
that there is a code of conduct

00:57:06.120 --> 00:57:09.270
and certain rules are
expected to be followed.

00:57:09.270 --> 00:57:11.700
Everybody gets one warning.

00:57:11.700 --> 00:57:15.150
They may not understand
that they've transgressed.

00:57:15.150 --> 00:57:18.840
After that, yes,
absolutely, shut them down.

00:57:18.840 --> 00:57:21.000
Mute them and leave the muted.

00:57:21.000 --> 00:57:23.130
Disable their
access to the talk.

00:57:23.130 --> 00:57:26.790
I recommend that you don't
kick them out of the room

00:57:26.790 --> 00:57:30.480
so that they're unable to see
your presentation because then

00:57:30.480 --> 00:57:32.730
they might go and
complain to somebody.

00:57:32.730 --> 00:57:37.560
But take away their ability to
impede other people's learning

00:57:37.560 --> 00:57:38.520
experience.

00:57:38.520 --> 00:57:41.480
As soon as somebody is a
troll, your responsibility

00:57:41.480 --> 00:57:45.680
is no longer to them, it's to
all of your other learners.

